// Template for `iosApp/Config/Secrets.xcconfig`, which is git-ignored.
//
// Copy this file to `Secrets.xcconfig` in the same directory and fill in the
// real values. Base.xcconfig `#include?`s it — the `?` means an absent file is
// not an error, so staging builds work on a fresh clone with no setup.
//
// Names here are environment-QUALIFIED, and deliberately so. The environment
// files include Base.xcconfig (and therefore this file) at their top, so their
// own later assignments beat anything named identically — a plain
// `STATIONLY_API_KEY` here would always lose to the placeholder in
// Production.xcconfig. Instead Production.xcconfig reads this value through
// `$(STATIONLY_API_KEY_PRODUCTION:default=…)`, so the placeholder applies only
// when this file is genuinely absent.
//
// ── Scope note ──
// This is about not committing production credentials to git. It is NOT about
// secrecy on the device: any API key inside a shipped iOS binary is
// extractable, whichever route it travels. The backend should treat this key
// as an identifier, not an authenticator.

// The production backend API key, sent as the API-key header to
// api.stationly.co.uk. Mirrors Android's `prod.STATIONLY_API_KEY` in
// local.properties.
//
// Staging's key is NOT here: it is committed in Staging.xcconfig, because it
// has been in this repo's git history (hardcoded in Platform.ios.kt) since the
// iOS app existed and there is nothing left to protect.
STATIONLY_API_KEY_PRODUCTION = paste-the-production-api-key-here
